Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix E - File formats</H1>
<HR>
This appendix describes the files the book's applications write and read: their own formats, and the parts
of the vCard, CSV and iCalendar formats they swap with other programs. It is what the Style Guide asks for
("document your file format"), and what you'd need to write another program that reads the same files.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Characters</H2>
RISC OS text is <I>Latin-1</I>: one byte for each character, with the accented letters of western European
languages above 127. Most files from other computers are <I>UTF-8</I>, which uses two or more bytes for
anything beyond plain ASCII. The applications:<P>
<UL>
<LI>write their own files as Latin-1 text (the desktop's usual way);<P>
<LI>write vCard, CSV and iCalendar files as UTF-8 (<CODE>encode</CODE> in <CODE>Formats</CODE>), because
that is what other programs expect;<P>
<LI>read those files as UTF-8 if they are valid UTF-8, and as Latin-1 if not; characters that aren't in
Latin-1 become <CODE>?</CODE> (<CODE>decode</CODE>).<P>
</UL>
Lines end with CR LF (<CODE>\r\n</CODE>) in vCard, CSV and iCalendar files; either kind of line ending is
read.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Contacts files (&amp;1C4)</H2>
A Contacts file is JSON text (file type &amp;1C4, <I>Contacts</I>), laid out over several lines:<P>
<PRE>
{
 "format": "Contacts",
 "version": 1,
 "contacts": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "title": "Mrs",
   "first": "Ann",
   "last": "Smith",
   "address": "12 Station Road\nCambridge",
   "postcode": "CB1 2JD",
   "home": "01223 555123",
   "birthday": "1968-03-14",
   "category": "Friends"
  },
  ...
 ]
}
</PRE>
<CODE>format</CODE> must be <CODE>"Contacts"</CODE>; <CODE>version</CODE> is 1. A program reading a later
version should refuse it (or read what it understands); the book's programs check <CODE>format</CODE> but
not yet <CODE>version</CODE> - adding that is a good exercise. Each contact has a whole-number
<CODE>id</CODE>, unique in the file, and these text fields; a field the user left empty is saved as an empty
string, <CODE>""</CODE>, and a program reading the file should treat a missing field the same way:<P>
<PRE>
title  first  last  company  job
address        several lines, separated by \n
postcode
work  home  mobile          telephone numbers
email  web
birthday       YYYY-MM-DD
category       Family, Friends, Work or Other
notes          several lines
</PRE>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Organiser files (&amp;1C5)</H2>
An Organiser file (file type &amp;1C5) is JSON too, holding every section of the organiser:<P>
<PRE>
{
 "format": "Organiser",
 "version": 1,
 "diary": [ ...appointments ],
 "todo": [ ...things to do ],
 "contacts": [ ...contacts, as in a Contacts file ],
 "notes": [ ...notepad pages ],
 "planner": [ ...planner bars ],
 "anniversaries": [ ...anniversaries ],
 "links": [ ...links ]
}
</PRE>
Every entry has an <CODE>id</CODE>, unique within its section. Days are written <CODE>YYYY-MM-DD</CODE>
and times <CODE>HH:MM</CODE> (24-hour, the organiser's own local time); an empty string means "none".<P>
<DL>
<DT>An appointment
<DD><PRE>
{ "id": 6, "date": "2026-09-24", "time": "14:15",
  "end": "14:45", "text": "Dentist", "alarm": 60,
  "repeat": null }
</PRE>
<CODE>time</CODE> <CODE>""</CODE> is an all-day entry; <CODE>alarm</CODE> is minutes before, or null;
<CODE>repeat</CODE> is null or <CODE>{ "every": "day" | "week" | "month" | "year", "count": n,
"until": "YYYY-MM-DD" }</CODE> (<CODE>count</CODE> and <CODE>until</CODE> may be null: for ever). A
repeating appointment is stored once: the days it falls on are worked out when they're shown.<P>
<DT>A thing to do
<DD><PRE>
{ "id": 1, "text": "Renew the car insurance", "priority": 1,
  "start": "", "due": "2026-09-28", "category": "Home",
  "done": false }
</PRE>
Priority 1 is the highest, 3 the lowest.<P>
<DT>A notepad page
<DD><CODE>{ "id": 1, "title": "Shopping list", "order": 0, "text": "Bread\nMilk" }</CODE>:
<CODE>order</CODE> is its place in the contents, from 0.<P>
<DT>A planner bar
<DD><CODE>{ "id": 1, "key": 0, "from": "2026-08-01", "to": "2026-08-14" }</CODE>: <CODE>key</CODE> is the
category, 0 to 5: Holiday, Training, Meeting, Project A, Project B, Away.<P>
<DT>An anniversary
<DD><CODE>{ "id": 1, "date": "1968-03-14", "text": "Ann's birthday" }</CODE>: the year is when it began,
so the organiser can say which anniversary it is.<P>
<DT>A link
<DD><CODE>{ "id": 1, "a": { "section": "diary", "id": 7 }, "b": { "section": "address", "id": 2 } }</CODE>:
two entries, each named by its section (<CODE>diary</CODE>, <CODE>todo</CODE>, <CODE>address</CODE>,
<CODE>notes</CODE>, <CODE>planner</CODE>, <CODE>anniversary</CODE>) and id.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>vCard</H2>
vCard (RFC 2426 for version 3.0) is how address books everywhere swap contacts. A file holds one card after
another; each line is a property's name, perhaps some parameters after semicolons, a colon, and its value.
<CODE>vcardWrite</CODE> writes version 3.0:<P>
<PRE>
BEGIN:VCARD
VERSION:3.0
N:Smith;Ann;;Mrs;
FN:Ann Smith
ORG:Acorn Pies Ltd
TITLE:Baker
TEL;TYPE=WORK,VOICE:01223 555777
TEL;TYPE=HOME,VOICE:01223 555123
TEL;TYPE=CELL:07700 900123
EMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET:ann.smith@example.com
ADR;TYPE=HOME:;;12 Station Road\nCambridge;;;CB1 2JD;
URL:http://example.com
BDAY:1968-03-14
NOTE:Met at the choir
CATEGORIES:Friends
END:VCARD
</PRE>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>N</CODE> has five parts: surname; first name; other names; title; suffix. <CODE>ADR</CODE> has
seven: post office box; extended address; street; town; region; postcode; country. The address goes in the
street part.<P>
<LI>In values, <CODE>\</CODE>, <CODE>,</CODE>, <CODE>;</CODE> and new lines are written
<CODE>\\</CODE>, <CODE>\,</CODE>, <CODE>\;</CODE> and <CODE>\n</CODE>.<P>
<LI>A line longer than 75 <I>octets</I> (bytes, as the file is written in UTF-8, where a character above
127 takes two) is <I>folded</I>: broken, with the rest on the next line after a space. <CODE>Formats</CODE>'
<CODE>fold</CODE> does it, for vCard and iCalendar alike.<P>
<LI>Empty properties are left out.<P>
</UL>
<CODE>vcardRead</CODE> reads version 2.1 and 3.0 cards:<P>
<UL>
<LI>folded lines are joined up again; <CODE>QUOTED-PRINTABLE</CODE> values (version 2.1:
<CODE>=0D=0A</CODE>, and soft line breaks: a line ending <CODE>=</CODE> goes on on the next) are decoded,
as UTF-8 if the property says <CODE>CHARSET=UTF-8</CODE>. Only those values are joined at a final
<CODE>=</CODE>: other values, such as a picture in base64, may end with one;<P>
<LI>a group name before the property (<CODE>item1.EMAIL</CODE>) is ignored;<P>
<LI><CODE>TEL</CODE> with <CODE>CELL</CODE> is the mobile, with <CODE>HOME</CODE> the home number,
anything else except <CODE>FAX</CODE> the work number (types written either way:
<CODE>TYPE=HOME</CODE> or plain <CODE>HOME</CODE>); the first of each kind is kept;<P>
<LI><CODE>ADR</CODE>'s street, town, region and country become the address's lines, and its postcode the
postcode;<P>
<LI><CODE>BDAY</CODE> may be <CODE>19680314</CODE> or <CODE>1968-03-14</CODE>;
<CODE>CATEGORIES</CODE> keeps the first;<P>
<LI>a card with no name in <CODE>N</CODE> uses <CODE>FN</CODE> as the first name.<P>
</UL>
Contacts saves vCard files as Text (&amp;FFF), named <CODE>.../vcf</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>CSV</H2>
CSV (comma-separated values, RFC 4180) is a table as text, which spreadsheets and databases read and write.
<CODE>csvWrite</CODE> writes a header row of titles, then one row for each contact, the fields in this
order:<P>
<PRE>
Title,First name,Surname,Company,Job title,Address,Postcode,
Work phone,Home phone,Mobile,Email,Web,Birthday,Category,Notes
</PRE>
(one line in the file). A cell containing a comma, a quote or a new line is put in double quotes, with any
quote in it doubled:<P>
<PRE>
Mrs,Ann,Smith,,,"12 Station Road
Cambridge",CB1 2JD,,01223 555123,...
,,,"Jam ""Tomorrow"" Cafe",,,,,,,...
</PRE>
<CODE>csvRead</CODE> takes the first row as the titles and matches each column to a field by its title
(ignoring capitals, spaces and punctuation) or by the field's own name (<CODE>first</CODE>,
<CODE>last</CODE> ...); columns it doesn't know are ignored, as are empty rows. Contacts saves CSV files as
type &amp;DFE (CSV), named <CODE>.../csv</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>iCalendar</H2>
iCalendar (RFC 5545) is how calendar programs swap appointments and things to do. <CODE>icalWrite</CODE>
writes a <CODE>VCALENDAR</CODE> holding a <CODE>VEVENT</CODE> for each appointment and a
<CODE>VTODO</CODE> for each thing to do:<P>
<PRE>
BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//RISC OS//Organiser//EN
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1790352000000-1@organiser
DTSTAMP:20260925T093000Z
DTSTART:20260924T141500
DTEND:20260924T144500
SUMMARY:Dentist
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Dentist
TRIGGER:-PT60M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1790352000000-2@organiser
DTSTAMP:20260925T093000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260128
SUMMARY:Pay the rent
RRULE:FREQ=MONTHLY
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:1790352000000-3@organiser
DTSTAMP:20260925T093000Z
SUMMARY:Renew the car insurance
PRIORITY:1
STATUS:NEEDS-ACTION
DUE;VALUE=DATE:20260928
CATEGORIES:Home
END:VTODO
END:VCALENDAR
</PRE>
<UL>
<LI>Every <CODE>VEVENT</CODE> and <CODE>VTODO</CODE> has a <CODE>DTSTAMP</CODE>, as the standard requires:
when the file was written, in UTC (it ends <CODE>Z</CODE>).<P>
<LI>Times have no time zone: they are the organiser's own local times (<CODE>20260924T141500</CODE>). An
all-day appointment has <CODE>DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:</CODE> and just the day.<P>
<LI>Repeats: <CODE>RRULE:FREQ=</CODE> <CODE>DAILY</CODE>, <CODE>WEEKLY</CODE>, <CODE>MONTHLY</CODE> or
<CODE>YEARLY</CODE>. A repeat that ends on a day has <CODE>;UNTIL=</CODE> in the same form as
<CODE>DTSTART</CODE> - just the day for an all-day appointment, the day and 23:59 for one with a time -
and one that ends after a number of times has <CODE>;COUNT=</CODE>; never both, as the standard asks. Other
parts of a repeat rule, and exceptions, aren't used.<P>
<LI>An alarm is a <CODE>VALARM</CODE> whose <CODE>TRIGGER</CODE> is written <CODE>-PT</CODE>minutes<CODE>M</CODE>
before the start. Reading, any length of time before the start is understood - <CODE>-PT1H</CODE>,
<CODE>-P1DT2H</CODE>, <CODE>-P1W</CODE> - and turned into minutes; a trigger at or after the start (or at
a fixed time) becomes 0.<P>
<LI>Priorities: iCalendar has 1 (highest) to 9, and 0 for none. The organiser writes its 1, 2 and 3 as 1,
5 and 9, and reads 1-3 as 1, 4-6 as 2, 7-9 as 3, and 0 as 2.<P>
<LI><CODE>STATUS:COMPLETED</CODE> is a thing done; <CODE>NEEDS-ACTION</CODE> one still to do.<P>
<LI>Text is escaped as in vCard, and long lines folded at 75 octets, as vCard's are.<P>
</UL>
<CODE>icalRead</CODE> reads these properties from any iCalendar file and ignores everything else (time
zones, attendees, other components). Appointments without a date are left out.<P>

